#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:2
msgid "For Django and Django REST Framework users"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:6
msgid "General concepts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:9
msgid "Layered configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:11
msgid ""
"Litestar uses a layered architecture. The parts used to group routes can "
"also be used to hierarchically organize an application. Settings and "
"configuration like dependencies, exception handlers, guards, middleware, "
"response cookies and headers, lifecycle hooks, OpenAPI configuration and "
"many more can be defined on any layer, and will be merged upon registration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:17
msgid ""
"The layers in hierarchical order are 1. Application (``Litestar``) 2. "
"``Router`` / ``Controller`` (these are on the same level and can be "
"arbitrarily nested) 3. Handler (``BaseRouteHandler``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:22
msgid ""
"When the same configuration is set on multiple layers, the value set closest "
"to the handler takes precedence. Django has no direct equivalent: settings "
"are global, middleware is global, and DRF's ``permission_classes`` / "
"``authentication_classes`` / ``throttle_classes`` are configured per view. "
"In Litestar these are configurable at every layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:29
msgid "Library-agnostic modelling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:31
msgid ""
"DRF uses ``Serializer`` and ``ModelSerializer`` for request validation, "
"response rendering, and database-to-response mapping; Django uses ``Form`` "
"for HTML form workflows. Litestar is mostly agnostic about the modelling "
"library. Internally it uses `msgspec <https://msgspec.dev>`_, and ships "
"first-class support for Pydantic, attrs, dataclasses, and ``TypedDict``\\ s "
"through its plugin system (:class:`~litestar.plugins.SerializationPlugin` "
"and :class:`~litestar.plugins.OpenAPISchemaPlugin`). The body of a request "
"is bound to a ``data`` parameter; the return annotation drives the response "
"shape. DTOs sit on top when the wire shape needs to diverge from the model "
"(see :ref:`onboarding/django:Serializers and DTOs`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:44
msgid "Application configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:46
msgid ""
"Django reads ``DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`` and loads a ``settings.py`` module; "
"DRF reads the ``REST_FRAMEWORK`` dict from the same module. Litestar is "
"configured through keyword arguments on the :class:`~litestar.app.Litestar` "
"constructor. There is no settings-module convention, but usually the factory "
"pattern is used, allowing to configure applications dynamically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:0 ../../../onboarding/django.rst:1560
msgid "Django / DRF"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:58
msgid ""
"# settings.py\n"
"DEBUG = True\n"
"INSTALLED_APPS = [\"django.contrib.contenttypes\", \"rest_framework\", "
"\"myapp\"]\n"
"MIDDLEWARE = [\"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\"]\n"
"ROOT_URLCONF = \"myapp.urls\"\n"
"DATABASES = {\"default\": {\"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.sqlite3\", "
"\"NAME\": \"db.sqlite3\"}}\n"
"\n"
"REST_FRAMEWORK = {\n"
"    \"DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES\": [\"rest_framework.authentication."
"SessionAuthentication\"],\n"
"    \"DEFAULT_PERMISSION_CLASSES\": [\"rest_framework.permissions."
"IsAuthenticated\"],\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:0 ../../../onboarding/django.rst:1560
msgid "Litestar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:75
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar\n"
"\n"
"def create_app(debug: bool = False) -> Litestar:\n"
"    return Litestar(\n"
"        route_handlers=[...],\n"
"        middleware=[...],\n"
"        plugins=[...],\n"
"        openapi_config=...,\n"
"        debug=debug,\n"
"    )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:87
msgid ""
"Plugins replace ``INSTALLED_APPS``: an ORM integration, a metrics "
"integration, or a custom feature ships as a plugin and is registered on the "
"application through the ``plugins`` keyword. See :ref:`onboarding/django:"
"Concepts without a direct equivalent` for the broader mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:94
msgid "Route handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:96
msgid ""
"Django wires URL patterns through ``urls.py`` and references view callables "
"or class-based views; DRF adds ``APIView``, ``GenericAPIView``, and "
"``ViewSet`` on top. Litestar uses an HTTP-method decorator on a function - "
"or a method on a :class:`~litestar.controller.Controller` - and registers "
"handlers on a :class:`~litestar.app.Litestar` or :class:`~litestar.router."
"Router` instance. The URL pattern lives on the decorator, not in a separate "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:0
msgid "Django"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:109
msgid ""
"# views.py\n"
"from django.http import JsonResponse\n"
"\n"
"def index(request):\n"
"    return JsonResponse({\"hello\": \"world\"})\n"
"\n"
"# urls.py\n"
"from django.urls import path\n"
"from . import views\n"
"\n"
"urlpatterns = [path(\"\", views.index)]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:0
msgid "DRF"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:126
msgid ""
"# views.py\n"
"from rest_framework.decorators import api_view\n"
"from rest_framework.response import Response\n"
"\n"
"@api_view([\"GET\"])\n"
"def index(request):\n"
"    return Response({\"hello\": \"world\"})\n"
"\n"
"# urls.py same as above"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:141
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def index() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[index])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:144
msgid ""
"Router and Controllers can be arbitrarily nested, but disappear at runtime. "
"When you register a controller or router on an application, they get reduced "
"to standard, independent route handlers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:148
msgid "For example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:151
msgid ""
"class UserController(Controller):\n"
"    path = \"/user\"\n"
"    guards = [can_read_user]\n"
"\n"
"    @get(\"{user_id:str}\", guards=[can_read_user])\n"
"    async def get_user(self, user_id: FromPath[str]) -> User:\n"
"        ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:160
msgid "is functionally equivalent to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:163
msgid ""
"@get(\"/user/{user_id:str}\", guards=[can_read_user])\n"
"async def get_user(user_id: FromPath[str]) -> User:\n"
"    ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:172
msgid ""
":ref:`Routing - Registering Routes <usage/routing/overview:registering "
"routes>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:176
msgid "Controllers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:178
msgid ""
"DRF's ``APIView``, ``GenericAPIView`` and ``ViewSet`` group HTTP methods on "
"a single class. Litestar's :class:`~litestar.controller.Controller` does the "
"same. Subclass ``Controller``, set ``path``, and decorate methods with the "
"HTTP method decorators. The difference is that DRF views dispatch by method "
"name, while Litestar uses the same decorator patterns as for standalone "
"routes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:190
msgid ""
"from rest_framework.views import APIView\n"
"from rest_framework.response import Response\n"
"\n"
"class ItemView(APIView):\n"
"    def get(self, request, item_id):\n"
"        return Response({\"id\": item_id})\n"
"\n"
"    def post(self, request):\n"
"        return Response(request.data, status=201)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:205
msgid ""
"from litestar import Controller, Litestar, delete, get, patch, post\n"
"from litestar.params import FromPath\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class ItemController(Controller):\n"
"    path = \"/items\"\n"
"\n"
"    @get(\"/\")\n"
"    async def list_items(self) -> list[dict[str, int]]:\n"
"        return [{\"id\": 1}, {\"id\": 2}]\n"
"\n"
"    @post(\"/\")\n"
"    async def create_item(self, data: dict[str, str]) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"        return data\n"
"\n"
"    @get(\"/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"    async def get_item(self, item_id: FromPath[int]) -> dict[str, int]:\n"
"        return {\"id\": item_id}\n"
"\n"
"    @patch(\"/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"    async def update_item(self, item_id: FromPath[int], data: dict[str, "
"str]) -> dict[str, object]:\n"
"        return {\"id\": item_id, **data}\n"
"\n"
"    @delete(\"/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"    async def delete_item(self, item_id: FromPath[int]) -> None:\n"
"        return None\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[ItemController])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:207
msgid ""
"A ``Controller`` carries the same layered configuration as a ``Router``: "
"dependencies, guards, middleware, exception handlers, and OpenAPI tags can "
"all be declared on the class and inherited by its methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:212
msgid ""
"Controllers are just a cosmetic layers on top of a router. Under the hood, a "
"controller instance will be converted into a router when it's registered on "
"the application"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:219
msgid ":doc:`/usage/routing/handlers`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:223
msgid "Routers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:225
msgid ""
"Django's ``include(\"app.urls\")`` and DRF's ``SimpleRouter()."
"register(...)`` mount a sub-tree of URLs under a prefix. Litestar's :class:"
"`~litestar.router.Router` takes a ``path`` and a list of ``route_handlers``, "
"which can themselves be handlers, controllers, or other routers. Routers "
"nest arbitrarily."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:236
msgid ""
"# myapp/urls.py\n"
"from django.urls import path\n"
"from . import views\n"
"\n"
"urlpatterns = [\n"
"    path(\"items/\", views.list_items),\n"
"    path(\"items/<int:item_id>/\", views.get_item),\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"# project/urls.py\n"
"from django.urls import include, path\n"
"\n"
"urlpatterns = [path(\"api/v1/\", include(\"myapp.urls\"))]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:255
msgid ""
"from rest_framework.routers import SimpleRouter\n"
"\n"
"router = SimpleRouter()\n"
"router.register(r\"items\", ItemViewSet, basename=\"item\")\n"
"\n"
"urlpatterns = [path(\"api/v1/\", include(router.urls))]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:267
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, Router, get\n"
"from litestar.params import FromPath\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def list_items() -> list[dict[str, int]]:\n"
"    return [{\"id\": 1}]\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"async def get_item(item_id: FromPath[int]) -> dict[str, int]:\n"
"    return {\"id\": item_id}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"items_router = Router(path=\"/items\", route_handlers=[list_items, "
"get_item])\n"
"api_router = Router(path=\"/api/v1\", route_handlers=[items_router])\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[api_router])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:272
msgid "Application state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:274
msgid ""
"Django stores process-global state in module-level variables or on ``django."
"apps.apps``. Litestar exposes :class:`~litestar.datastructures.State`, "
"seeded on the application and available to dependencies through the "
"``state`` parameter and to handlers through ``request.app.state``. Per-"
"request state lives on ``request.state`` instead and inherits from the "
"application state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:286
msgid ""
"# myapp/apps.py\n"
"from django.apps import AppConfig\n"
"\n"
"class MyAppConfig(AppConfig):\n"
"    def ready(self):\n"
"        from .clients import init_redis\n"
"        init_redis()\n"
"\n"
"# services.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:302
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.datastructures import State\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class RedisClient:\n"
"    \"\"\"Stand-in for a configured Redis client.\"\"\"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def handler(state: State) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    redis: RedisClient = state.redis\n"
"    return {\"client\": type(redis).__name__}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(\n"
"    route_handlers=[handler],\n"
"    state=State({\"redis\": RedisClient()}),\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:307
msgid "Sync and async handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:309
msgid ""
"Django views and DRF views are synchronous by default; Django 4+ supports "
"``async def`` views through an in-process sync-to-async bridge. The bridge "
"cuts both ways: a sync view runs on a thread pool from the ASGI worker, an "
"``async def`` view runs on the event loop, and ORM / cache / middleware "
"calls made from an async view are wrapped in ``sync_to_async`` to bounce "
"back to a thread. DRF itself is still sync-only: serializers, throttles, and "
"the ``APIView`` dispatch loop do not have async equivalents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:317
msgid ""
"Litestar inverts the default. Handlers are ``async def`` and run directly on "
"the event loop. There is no implicit thread bridge: a blocking call in an "
"async handler blocks the worker. A ``def`` handler must declare which side "
"it runs on:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:322
msgid ""
"``sync_to_thread=True`` offloads the call to a thread pool; Use when porting "
"blocking ORM code, file I/O, or a third-party library without async support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:325
msgid ""
"``sync_to_thread=False`` runs the call inline on the event loop: reserved "
"for short, non-blocking work where the offload would cost more than the call "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:329
msgid ""
"A bare ``def`` handler with neither flag emits a deprecation warning at "
"startup: the choice is load-bearing enough that the framework asks you to be "
"explicit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:339
msgid ""
"def slow_view(request):\n"
"    time.sleep(0.01)  # implicitly runs in the request thread\n"
"    return JsonResponse({\"hello\": \"world\"})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:348
msgid ""
"import time\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\", sync_to_thread=True)\n"
"def slow_handler() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    time.sleep(0.01)\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/fast\", sync_to_thread=False)\n"
"def fast_handler() -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"hello\": \"world\"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[slow_handler, fast_handler])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:352
msgid "Handlers and request data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:354
msgid ""
"DRF deserialises the body once on the view level and exposes ``request."
"data``, ``request.query_params``, ``request.GET``, ``request.POST``, "
"``request.FILES``, and ``request.COOKIES``; Django's ``HttpRequest`` exposes "
"the lower-level versions of the same. Litestar injects each piece through "
"typed handler parameters using :class:`~typing.Annotated` and ``From*`` "
"markers, so the handler signature describes the contract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:363
msgid "Path and query parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:365
msgid ""
"Django converts path parameters with the path-converter syntax (``<int:"
"item_id>``) and exposes them as positional arguments; query parameters come "
"from ``request.GET.get(...)``. DRF exposes them through ``self.kwargs`` and "
"``request.query_params``. Litestar uses path-converter syntax in the "
"decorator and binds the values through :data:`~litestar.params.FromPath` "
"and :data:`~litestar.params.FromQuery`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:378
msgid ""
"# urls.py\n"
"path(\"items/<int:item_id>/\", views.get_item)\n"
"\n"
"# views.py\n"
"def get_item(request, item_id):\n"
"    limit = int(request.GET.get(\"limit\", \"10\"))\n"
"    return JsonResponse({\"id\": item_id, \"limit\": limit})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:391
msgid ""
"class ItemView(APIView):\n"
"    def get(self, request, item_id):\n"
"        limit = int(request.query_params.get(\"limit\", \"10\"))\n"
"        return Response({\"id\": item_id, \"limit\": limit})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:401
msgid ""
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.params import FromPath, FromQuery\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/items/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"async def get_item(item_id: FromPath[int], limit: FromQuery[int]) -> "
"dict[str, int]:\n"
"    return {\"id\": item_id, \"limit\": limit}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[get_item])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:403
msgid ""
"To attach validation constraints or OpenAPI metadata, use the :class:"
"`~typing.Annotated` form with :class:`~litestar.params.PathParameter` and :"
"class:`~litestar.params.QueryParameter`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:413
msgid ""
"class LimitSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n"
"    limit = serializers.IntegerField(min_value=1, max_value=100)\n"
"\n"
"class ItemView(APIView):\n"
"    def get(self, request, item_id):\n"
"        serializer = LimitSerializer(data=request.query_params)\n"
"        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n"
"        ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:427
msgid ""
"from typing import Annotated\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.params import PathParameter, QueryParameter\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/items/{item_id:int}\")\n"
"async def get_item(\n"
"    item_id: Annotated[int, PathParameter(gt=0)],\n"
"    limit: Annotated[int, QueryParameter(gt=0, le=100)],\n"
") -> dict[str, int]:\n"
"    return {\"id\": item_id, \"limit\": limit}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[get_item])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:431
msgid ":doc:`/usage/routing/parameters`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:435
msgid "Header and cookie parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:437
msgid ""
"DRF reads headers from ``request.headers`` and cookies from ``request."
"COOKIES``. Litestar binds them through :class:`~litestar.params."
"HeaderParameter` and :class:`~litestar.params.CookieParameter` (or their "
"generic forms :data:`~litestar.params.FromHeader` and :data:`~litestar."
"params.FromCookie`). When the cookie or header name does not match the "
"Python parameter name, pass ``name=`` explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:450
msgid ""
"class SecureView(APIView):\n"
"    def get(self, request):\n"
"        session_id = request.COOKIES[\"sessionid\"]\n"
"        api_key = request.headers[\"X-API-Key\"]\n"
"        return Response({\"session\": session_id, \"api_key\": api_key})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:461
msgid ""
"from typing import Annotated\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, get\n"
"from litestar.params import CookieParameter, HeaderParameter\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@get(\"/\")\n"
"async def handler(\n"
"    session_id: Annotated[str, CookieParameter(name=\"sessionid\")],\n"
"    api_key: Annotated[str, HeaderParameter(name=\"X-API-Key\")],\n"
") -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"session\": session_id, \"api_key\": api_key}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[handler])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:465
msgid "JSON request body"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:467
msgid ""
"DRF's parser populates ``request.data`` and a ``Serializer`` validates the "
"dict into a Python object. Litestar injects the validated body through a "
"``data`` parameter typed against any supported model (dataclasses , msgspec :"
"class:`msgspec.Struct`, Pydantic model, attrs class). Validation runs before "
"the handler is called; an invalid body raises :class:`~litestar.exceptions."
"ValidationException` which produces a `400 - Bad Request` response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:480
msgid ""
"class ItemSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n"
"    name = serializers.CharField()\n"
"    price = serializers.FloatField()\n"
"\n"
"class ItemView(APIView):\n"
"    def post(self, request):\n"
"        serializer = ItemSerializer(data=request.data)\n"
"        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n"
"        return Response(serializer.validated_data, status=201)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:495
msgid ""
"from dataclasses import dataclass\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, post\n"
"from litestar.params import JSONBody\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@dataclass\n"
"class Item:\n"
"    name: str\n"
"    price: float\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@post(\"/items\")\n"
"async def create_item(data: JSONBody[Item]) -> Item:\n"
"    return data\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[create_item])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:497
msgid ""
"Field-level renaming, partial updates, and write-vs-read separation move to "
"the DTO layer. See :ref:`onboarding/django:Serializers and DTOs`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:502 ../../../onboarding/django.rst:1578
msgid "Form data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:504
msgid ""
"Django's ``Form`` and DRF's ``MultiPartParser`` / ``FormParser`` populate "
"``request.POST``. Litestar uses the same ``data`` parameter with the :data:"
"`~litestar.params.URLEncodedBody` marker for form-encoded bodies, or :data:"
"`~litestar.params.MultipartBody` for multipart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:515
msgid ""
"from django.views.decorators.http import require_POST\n"
"\n"
"@require_POST\n"
"def login(request):\n"
"    username = request.POST[\"username\"]\n"
"    password = request.POST[\"password\"]\n"
"    return JsonResponse({\"user\": username})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:528
msgid ""
"from dataclasses import dataclass\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, post\n"
"from litestar.params import URLEncodedBody\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@dataclass\n"
"class LoginForm:\n"
"    username: str\n"
"    password: str\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@post(\"/login\")\n"
"async def login(data: URLEncodedBody[LoginForm]) -> dict[str, str]:\n"
"    return {\"user\": data.username}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[login])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:532
msgid "File uploads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:534
msgid ""
"Django exposes uploaded files through ``request.FILES`` (an ``UploadedFile`` "
"per field). Litestar uses :class:`~litestar.datastructures.UploadFile`, or "
"``list[UploadFile]`` for multiple files under the same field name, with a :"
"data:`~litestar.params.MultipartBody` marker:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:545
msgid ""
"def upload(request):\n"
"    files = request.FILES.getlist(\"data\")\n"
"    return JsonResponse({\"file_names\": [f.name for f in files]})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:0
msgid "Python 3.8+"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../onboarding/django.rst:554
msgid ""
"from typing import Dict, List, Optional\n"
"\n"
"from litestar import Litestar, post\n"
"from litestar.datastructures import UploadFile\n"
"from litestar.params import MultipartBody\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@post(\"/upload\")\n"
"async def upload_files(data: MultipartBody[List[UploadFile]]) -> Dict[str, "
"List[Optional[str]]]:\n"
"    return {\"file_names\": [file.filename for file in data]}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"app = Litestar(route_handlers=[upload_files])"
msgstr ""

